  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Time Expansion",
    "lesson_core_move": "Map each time feature in $x(t)$ to t0/a in $x(at)$ for $0<a<1 and identify the resulting waveform as expanded away from t=0 with unchanged amplitudes$.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Time Expansion",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time Expansion",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When you see $y(t)=x(at)$ with $0<a<1$, read it as time expansion. The waveform keeps the same amplitudes, but every time feature moves away from $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(at) \\quad 0<a<1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $x(t/2)$ as the clean base case. Here $a=\\tfrac{1}{2}$, so a feature that was at $t_0$ in $x(t)$ appears at $t_{new}=t_0/a=2t_0$ in $x(t/2)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t/2) \\qquad t_{new}=t_0/a",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The same $t_0/a$ rule applies to every other time feature, so the whole signal stretches horizontally. Only the time locations change; the heights stay the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Time Expansion",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Recognizing $x(t/2)$ as a Time Expansion",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Decide whether $x(t/2)$ is a time expansion or a time compression, and state the stretch factor.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the fraction inside the argument. In $x(t/2)$, rewrite the signal as $x(at)$ with $a=\\tfrac{1}{2}$. Because $0<a<1$, this is a time expansion.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a=\\tfrac{1}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now check one landmark time. If a feature was at $t_0$ in $x(t)$, then in $x(at)$ it appears where $at=t_0$, so $t_{new}=t_0/a$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t_{new}=t_0/a=2t_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So every time feature in the waveform moves twice as far from $t=0$ while its height stays the same. That is why $x(t/2)$ is a time expansion with stretch factor $2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Time Expansion",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Mapping Original Time Points to t0/a",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A feature is located at $t_0=-6$ in $x(t)$. If the signal becomes $x(t/3)$, where does that feature move, and what is the general rule for any $t_0$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the factor inside the argument. In $x(t/3)$, rewrite the signal as $x(at)$ with $a=\\tfrac{1}{3}$, so the location rule is $t_0 \\mapsto t_0/a$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a=\\tfrac{1}{3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply that rule to the labeled time point. Solving $at=t_0$ gives $(\\tfrac{1}{3})t=-6$, so the same feature moves to $t_{new}=t_0/a=-18$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t_{new}=t_0/a=-18",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The sign stays negative, but the point moves farther from $t=0$. That is the universal rule for this lesson: every original time point $t_0$ moves to $t_0/a$ when $0<a<1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Time Expansion",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Finding Expanded Support with Unchanged Amplitude",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)$ is nonzero on $[1,3]$ and has peak amplitude $5$, find the support of $x(t/2)$ and state what happens to the amplitude.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the factor inside the argument. In $x(t/2)$, rewrite the signal as $x(at)$ with $a=\\tfrac{1}{2}$. That means every original time point moves by the rule $t_{new}=t_0/a$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a=\\tfrac{1}{2},\\qquad t_{new}=t_0/a",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the rule to the support endpoints. The left endpoint $1$ moves to $1/(1/2)=2$, and the right endpoint $3$ moves to $3/(1/2)=6$, so the new support is $[2,6]$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "[1,3]\\ \\mapsto\\ [2,6]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The interval widens away from $t=0$, but the waveform heights do not change. So the support expands to $[2,6]$ and the peak amplitude stays $5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
